speeches.en:
Yes! Thank you, Honourable Speaker. I fully support this bill submitted today by an individual member. Law number 7/2010 (Act on Administering the Administrative Divisions of the Maldives in a Decentralised Manner

), I support this bill submitted to amend it. The main reason I support it is that what this bill sets out is an amendment proposed so that the Maldivian government, that is, whichever government is in office at the time, can take land from among the lands within an island's jurisdiction in order to carry out the development projects it needs. In reality, some councils, even if only a few councils, are carrying out this practice in this way. Because of that, development projects that are to be carried out for the convenience and development of the Maldivian people are not carried out and are delayed, and since things are going this way, I support this because I see that bringing this bill, this amendment, in this way would be beneficial. In reality, various people are speaking in various ways about this bill. As they do, what we notice is that the actual core issue is about taking a place, or an area of land, from land within an island's jurisdiction. When speaking about this, we also need to look at when this started being done this way and how it came about. Under the MDP government, in the first government formed, in 2008 and 2009, parts of the land of various islands of the Maldives were taken to be given out for various other purposes. Therefore, even then, in reality, this was a practice that was carried out. This is something that has been done. In reality, what is being attempted is not something new. Therefore, in reality, as I see it, the matters set out in this bill are very important. Also, regarding the land given out that way back then, for what purpose and why it was given out, there are also places that, because of differences of opinion between the council and the government concerning the parties who invested there, and among the reasons for those differences, could not be properly developed, could not be invested in, and have stalled. Therefore, I consider it important that this bill also addresses those matters, sets them out, and provides a way for those matters to move forward. In reality, when various councils on various islands of the Maldives designate land for various government purposes, I notice that in recent times they have set deadlines, you know. Given deadlines. That the government must start this project within such-and-such six months, or must start within one year. Therefore, when land is designated with deadlines given that way, when the government finances a project, in reality carrying it out becomes difficult. It becomes troublesome. Therefore, those matters too, as I believe, are something that must be taken into consideration at the committee stage of the bill, I say. And in addition, when major national development projects are carried out, those projects'

various, given how the Maldives is formed, there are various centres belonging to various regions. Therefore, deciding which place in each centre, which place in which region, is in reality also a government decision. Therefore, in reality, as I see it, bringing this amendment so that such matters can be taken into account is a very important amendment. What I wish is that at the committee stage this bill is considered in depth and finalised in an easy way that does not obstruct the convenience and development the Maldivian people stand to receive. Thank you very much for the opportunity.
